ath-academy-id "q-9378" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =8.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-12,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-12}{-2}=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-9378/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9378"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-9379/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "42585776-2bbc-5b70-93c2-27a06be87bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9379/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "8fae343c-b549-5782-bc82-7696bf1a15f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9379/selection/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" :answer/id #uuid "be9886af-aa6f-551a-b9e6-7550ddd82ea6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9379/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "8eddd128-5ed2-5db4-b165-4593c60ad786" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9379/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "c85c487b-d5f8-5f9c-9a74-cbe50bcb0ff8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9379/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b1de07-a02c-5fa6-88b7-ad011fd8506d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-9379/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-9379/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-9379/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-9379/selection/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" "q-9379/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-9379/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-9379" :question/id #uuid "9da8023e-5a11-5214-945f-fcb19dad739a" :question/math-academy-id "q-9379" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-9379/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9379"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107109/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "8a55330a-c6b5-596e-9bd3-215d3e714565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107109/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "1ce83ab5-5fd0-5212-aeaa-4600e8475062" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107109/selection/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" :answer/id #uuid "dd461dd9-eb77-5a0f-a2c4-471b9fdc812a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107109/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "8ff657c2-a6a8-50d0-80c5-8d9317adf5d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107109/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "916d857b-1ec8-5de9-84cf-f4b348c14580" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107109/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cdb7898-ae5a-5ad5-a58d-0aa795f1b6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107109/selection/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" :answer-field/choices ["q-107109/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-107109/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-107109/selection/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" "q-107109/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-107109/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107109" :question/id #uuid "7139a2cb-9937-5b21-8c2d-93c8866b4221" :question/math-academy-id "q-107109" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{4}{-3}=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-107109/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107109"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-18078/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a2d49bc0-c995-558c-bbaa-b2422a217f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18078/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "79711157-546c-5570-b8a9-8099c0e9a8da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18078/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "203dbb18-d7b1-52a7-a8bd-7b94beed8b8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18078/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "70347d74-e286-5708-9d99-cbf9ab9f3385" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18078/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "c764a7f0-29a8-5e43-b733-a08ff0b1ceee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18078/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9e53bd-dc07-5bfe-818f-47dd82570c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-18078/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-18078/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-18078/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-18078/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-18078/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-18078/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-18078" :question/id #uuid "a67b8237-505d-5006-b374-4fe4da363688" :question/math-academy-id "q-18078" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{6}{-2}=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-18078/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-18078"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-108039/