estion/math-academy-id "q-108039" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-108039/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108039"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80319/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer/id #uuid "60726f93-4f59-526b-8b9a-a051210bf35c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80319/selection/8b8a36d96bbf76ba1018298ac259106e51224db0adb10c038acbf29fba382784" :answer/id #uuid "f99d745d-5a46-516c-8636-383df7e5b8eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80319/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" :answer/id #uuid "27111d1f-e1a8-5afa-b872-e7abd4103a72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80319/selection/80f7643749639dc6ac3cb6ea590a80572731108334d6c4e6c17a301b44d6338a" :answer/id #uuid "03ef21f0-23ef-52e6-99a0-9319c25b19a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80319/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" :answer/id #uuid "0e6609f0-6e0c-5408-b591-7dd97ad93742" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80319/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "41b002dc-0e6f-533d-aafe-39f29f09fac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80319/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" :answer-field/choices ["q-80319/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" "q-80319/selection/8b8a36d96bbf76ba1018298ac259106e51224db0adb10c038acbf29fba382784" "q-80319/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" "q-80319/selection/80f7643749639dc6ac3cb6ea590a80572731108334d6c4e6c17a301b44d6338a" "q-80319/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80319" :question/id #uuid "507cd94a-0757-5af2-9b87-e9063e728920" :question/math-academy-id "q-80319" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80319/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80319"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80318/selection/80f7643749639dc6ac3cb6ea590a80572731108334d6c4e6c17a301b44d6338a" :answer/id #uuid "805f1a94-ac79-52c5-91dd-86d138a81f37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80318/selection/4afe72ce1ff2948bb282f72a051259b7c7c995391c6a8ae0e3d05b32a2584b91" :answer/id #uuid "042b47ba-8769-546e-9060-fbc9909c1a07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80318/selection/fa3f23c7334055936c2275cd39a879c618af1144bc7e7a75445f0ec46dcc7115" :answer/id #uuid "df57d1e8-51de-568d-b326-b4a04f0486c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80318/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" :answer/id #uuid "7e3e08ea-16b7-519d-b618-8615b7f4bde4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80318/selection/a58e880e036264e5b21442a2e9867416c984cde73a96371bb168dcb66090a333" :answer/id #uuid "cebf6534-f65a-53c0-8fe2-fe8792bc41a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80318/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "24e0b5e4-5643-5cdc-bc4d-fb8c357c52c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80318/selection/80f7643749639dc6ac3cb6ea590a80572731108334d6c4e6c17a301b44d6338a" :answer-field/choices ["q-80318/selection/80f7643749639dc6ac3cb6ea590a80572731108334d6c4e6c17a301b44d6338a" "q-80318/selection/4afe72ce1ff2948bb282f72a051259b7c7c995391c6a8ae0e3d05b32a2584b91" "q-80318/selection/fa3f23c7334055936c2275cd39a879c618af1144bc7e7a75445f0ec46dcc7115" "q-80318/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" "q-80318/selection/a58e880e036264e5b21442a2e9867416c984cde73a96371bb168dcb66090a333"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80318" :question/id #uuid "61d50a2f-9c10-50b7-9276-e49a32b1a6cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80318" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =6.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80318/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80318"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107273/selection/3d2d29ce4b7b903a916db999e24fe31ee6bcadca9c46f3055795770438854a8a" :answer/id #uuid "172bfd1e-f653-597c-b2ae-e9ad12b206c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107273/selection/cf2e1913ea77e749b28d4e110ef01eb46249d8bfd88881ac7db2f0c4e1652fd4" :answer/id #uuid "7b5bed77-5c8f-58a0-af7b-f7a77507423c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107273/selection/8434b13432a11804b2a9de7475dacec2ca8a24aade11115c16d8cacde7dbb9a3" :answer/id #uuid "0cc4d3b0-22ba-58ff-9028-13505e45a855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107273/selection/36c95da0dd2c224451f3c22833b919b832e27b21b36b623e0c08dacfeb482ab4" :answer/id #uuid "aa486a17-39b1-5bc2-b268-9fc8524f4b91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107273/selection/eb96bf9db1cf64cf4cfbc3f818f62446250830ffb07caa23f293765f421e5953" :answer/id #uuid "d5c8f5c2-4739-52c2-90b5-c169df095373" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107273/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f01c4a0b-211f-5d61-b6fa-10930143078e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107273/selection/cf2e1913ea77e749b28d4e110ef01eb46249d8bfd88881ac7db2f0c4e1652fd4" :answer-field/choices ["q-107273/selection/3d2d29ce4b7b903a916db999e24fe31ee6bcadca9c46f3055795770438854a8a" "q-107273/selection/cf2e1913ea77e749b28d4e110ef01eb46249d8bfd88881ac7db2f0c4e1652fd4" "q-107273/selection/8434b13432a11804b2a9de7475dacec2ca8a24aade11115c16d8cacde7dbb9a3" "q-107273/selection/36c95da0dd2c224451f3c22833b919b832e27b21b36b623e0c08dacfeb482ab4" "q-107273/selection/eb96bf9db1cf64cf4cfbc3f818f62446250830ffb07caa23f293765f421e5953"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107273" :question/id #uuid "d5d9e801-eca8-5d4b-8b13-1bb066d0bb23" :question/math-academy-id "q-107273" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}22 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}25{x}_{1}-40{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n25{x}_{1}-40{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-107273/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107273"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107140/selection/e96e1d3465e2c709d54a4bb8564b2bd9898dcadb3b73045259d0ca18ed338675" :answer/id #uuid "1e2f7b54-13eb-53c1-a52b-04e21c7aef37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107140/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer/id #uuid "97607ff3-2079-568b-acb7-93d3699f7f3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107140/selection/84762459bef6f107ef2cbf2e830c8f92da9b281e07c47ed0412a20ac8689c76a" :answer/id #uuid "1f39f90b-69a3-54b3-8d4d-f16d469148f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107140/selection/11b5a2b12f0fa978e3ea1c18ad8bf2352a69dfc21f078a6e3e3dfb882a45142d" :answer/id #uuid "0974c705-0c5f-5cac-afa0-6c1022fd1a1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107140/selection/09ea42bae490ea740c5bbb960aeb7b68bbdc139e76e388604763f512a33a12e1" :answer/id #uuid "f7219de7-6a35-514f-a572-5f315a77b556" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107140/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0d7695-d5c2-51ed-b7e2-cf5b7f9a3227" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107140/selection/09ea42bae490ea740c5bbb960aeb7b68bbdc139e76e388604763f512a33a12e1" :answer-field/choices ["q-107140/selection/e96e1d3465e2c709d54a4bb8564b2bd9898dcadb3b73045259d0ca18ed338675" "q-107140/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" "q-107140/selection/84762459bef6f107ef2cbf2e830c8f92da9b281e07c47ed0412a20ac8689c76a" "q-107140/selection/11b5a2b12f0fa978e3ea1c18ad8bf2352a69dfc21f078a6e3e3dfb882a45142d" "q-107140/selection/09ea42bae490ea740c5bbb960aeb7b68bbdc139e76e388604763f512a33a12e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107140" :question/id #uuid "9059fbc2-01af-5d93-8d2c-0c4d7f9f1b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107140" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n12{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-107140/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107140"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107138/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" :answer/id #uuid "e8977175-cc51-5b9d-a5bb-e97a844c566a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107138/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer/id #uuid "da5e26f5-9dce-50e9-92c2-cbac6f3772ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107138/selection/84762459bef6f107ef2cbf2e830c8f92da9b281e07c47ed0412a20ac8689c76a" :answer/id #uuid "bceeae97-5ba6-5b30-9196-7c723c04dddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107138/selection/0ea84eddbb86c801755798ff5359850de462b3ab59bf3f8027cfb439e37a1853" :answer/id #uuid "614b3066-4967-5fc0-b948-ca6693fc0d7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107138/selection/4afe72ce1ff2948bb282f72a051259b7c7c995391c6a8ae0e3d05b32a2584b91" :answer/id #uuid "e0ae2e16-1b1e-575f-aede-6a0d0b5fa7be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107138/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1742edd9-9118-5fef-849a-2d60ef55dd1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107138/selection/4afe72ce1ff2948bb282f72a051259b7c7c995391c6a8ae0e3d05b32a2584b91" :answer-field/choices ["q-107138/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" "q-107138/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" "q-107138/selection/84762459bef6f107ef2cbf2e830c8f92da9b281e07c47ed0412a20ac8689c76a" "q-107138/selection/0ea84eddbb86c801755798ff5359850de462b3ab59bf3f8027cfb439e37a1853" "q-107138/selection/4afe72ce1ff2948bb282f72a051259b7c7c995391c6a8ae0e3d05b32a2584b91"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107138" :question/id #uuid "607e3c10-52c5-5502-b67a-00410a83f5d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-107138" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-107138/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107138"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80324/selection/25ee1738fd674ddb230f3d3ab9a4210d459ee592c8bfa300fc497f9f51d9f541" :answer/id #uuid "9dddb4e9-a028-5206-9041-70fbcc3b64b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80324/selection/6aa186ae9dce487fba3dc72c9fc8224e7c647e95382fb0932f8011fa3cc54363" :answer/id #uuid "4168a2fd-17ca-563b-a51f-909264b0ef33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80324/selection/4518b703fcf4833dd7dcde4e42c2c90399fe9485e4b24ca16515880a563d4a12" :answer/id #uuid "cfaf9bbe-cbc0-5056-9936-8a8539cde044" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80324/selection/89db7277fbc6f945f3edf67c771e8658080ca754f83b05cf1cd8b4b85d7f8244" :answer/id #uuid "b5c52fdf-fcc6-54ee-9d54-ee53190b6dad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80324/selection/ec565b4d635a3f2e49eeaf17f49bb00347efb33b5db2f8a6e3799143c4db7822" :answer/id #uuid "b142183a-c6ef-51fc-9dca-ba4512f0a672" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80324/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "19dcd08b-331a-52e1-b962-d7de0871e0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80324/selection/25ee1738fd674ddb230f3d3ab9a4210d459ee592c8bfa300fc497f9f51d9f541" :answer-field/choices ["q-80324/selection/25ee1738fd674ddb230f3d3ab9a4210d459ee592c8bfa300fc497f9f51d9f541" "q-80324/selection/6aa186ae9dce487fba3dc72c9fc8224e7c647e95382fb0932f8011fa3cc54363" "q-80324/selection/4518b703fcf4833dd7dcde4e42c2c90399fe9485e4b24ca16515880a563d4a12" "q-80324/selection/89db7277fbc6f945f3edf67c771e8658080ca754f83b05cf1cd8b4b85d7f8244" "q-80324/selection/ec565b4d635a3f2e49eeaf17f49bb00347efb33b5db2f8a6e3799143c4db7822"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80324" :question/id #uuid "488c7744-4f7f-513d-943a-cfec041b364c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80324" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80324/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80324"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-9385/selection/031bdcb0edf991852e7c72252b63e0454cefff6e8deee79fc6fb3310485473eb" :answer/id #uuid "116d58d7-cd46-54db-b56a-2f8cfd6e1807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9385/selection/56473035e409f391ec981e462f515b68c196ebbcecdb8bc54fe68abc111c58d2" :answer/id #uuid "b5f82103-752f-5f6c-8080-28f6b48dc0a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9385/selection/79d10eb53b32bea96b7413b696b2164ee179de5b0aed0ad42c3856788389d69b" :answer/id #uuid "0e65b3b2-5197-51dc-9fc5-34b3d0962f9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9385/selection/92aade4453a9bff8cfbe91a8750345e1ef1a34bb02c074a4bdb33229d2915ba1" :answer/id #uuid "c1863b81-e9db-5d0b-996c-e795c1767161" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9385/selection/75229c2a99a69f17bee71d4ec40ac8784a163167c97d3419aa0c2dad84a7e2f6" :answer/id #uuid "6764647a-cdee-5869-a3d9-0f10deae489e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9385/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a486feb-feef-51b2-9d3c-e8bbe2eb6f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-9385/selection/92aade4453a9bff8cfbe91a8750345e1ef1a34bb02c074a4bdb33229d2915ba1" :answer-field/choices ["q-9385/selection/031bdcb0edf991852e7c72252b63e0454cefff6e8deee79fc6fb3310485473eb" "q-9385/selection/56473035e409f391ec981e462f515b68c196ebbcecdb8bc54fe68abc111c58d2" "q-9385/selection/79d10eb53b32bea96b7413b696b2164ee179de5b0aed0ad42c3856788389d69b" "q-9385/selection/92aade4453a9bff8cfbe91a8750345e1ef1a34bb02c074a4bdb33229d2915ba1" "q-9385/selection/75229c2a99a69f17bee71d4ec40ac8784a163167c97d3419aa0c2dad84a7e2f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-9385" :question/id #uuid "4fd5afae-51c2-5e00-a76d-3cddc5c13593" :question/math-academy-id "q-9385" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-9385/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9385"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-9383/selection/c808a655a5be001e76c6365fac50f0cac18645d84a870ec49624ba42596a504c" :answer/id #uuid "01852677-aadf-53e3-bc9e-690aadb9e69c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9383/selection/fe4f745c186115994f04e8e256a88ff74cf5d4f9b45c47490335c89690e37213" :answer/id #uuid "b56803ee-bcb7-5d64-951f-128d0774dba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9383/selection/cafc91f783a8d6c62644ff503e495f4dbad4c5948ef1829e1c912e65e14a1ce8" :answer/id #uuid "4ef6960f-0ac5-5fba-9e5a-38d6de991645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9383/selection/85c5bf0b322a16cc4fe97360525d67f9741e2d6daf0f17c20bb5737ed81034fb" :answer/id #uuid "0dd30d92-3c56-5e67-873b-cb18a0d9f3fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9383/selection/d22ca61cbaf363a470439c709483471f6add7a1acaf7c65397dacdc42a9f009d" :answer/id #uuid "f923a474-377c-5c34-8b1b-12738cd31668" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9383/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ec01e8-8cbd-585e-8ab0-c6b2aaee4103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-9383/selection/85c5bf0b322a16cc4fe97360525d67f9741e2d6daf0f17c20bb5737ed81034fb" :answer-field/choices ["q-9383/selection/c808a655a5be001e76c6365fac50f0cac18645d84a870ec49624ba42596a504c" "q-9383/selection/fe4f745c186115994f04e8e256a88ff74cf5d4f9b45c47490335c89690e37213" "q-9383/selection/cafc91f783a8d6c62644ff503e495f4dbad4c5948ef1829e1c912e65e14a1ce8" "q-9383/selection/85c5bf0b322a16cc4fe97360525d67f9741e2d6daf0f17c20bb5737ed81034fb" "q-9383/selection/d22ca61cbaf363a470439c709483471f6add7a1acaf7c65397dacdc42a9f009d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-9383" :question/id #uuid "d8fad342-fcb8-5d86-a71d-da0696219a20" :question/math-academy-id "q-9383" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-9383/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9383"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107311/selection/7ed8ed7aff999b62180a1081cd49990f18ba6bec256be9e62a2b2d4b9385e86a" :answer/id #uuid "c42618ed-fc70-5aef-b1ac-f68ca9a9b1b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107311/selection/79982823ad55938a9f7236929d695367b3b673bd6678f2f03e0ef53b14d590c3" :answer/id #uuid "676c9e22-19b5-5ae4-87f7-ffbafa0603fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107311/selection/bdf02b857655926a707d4d030c1f998d371456a097454b3311c924b538df362f" :answer/id #uuid "b98a5209-466c-5e41-b0dd-4da34f2960d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107311/selection/c2520e5f3e1d873a1f529de0067352587e320f2eb5c11f36a10589afa8fe72ac" :answer/id #uuid "979acb1b-22c7-5456-bac8-440b9870a015" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107311/selection/c5f992953d152b5e7cb0fdf7ddd228b1af5e5a8d4203d37d6fefbec1fad7aef9" :answer/id #uuid "9bd38705-a862-58b3-aafd-22b55470016f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107311/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe172c2-edd4-5668-b213-3f2856accb1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107311/selection/7ed8ed7aff999b62180a1081cd49990f18ba6bec256be9e62a2b2d4b9385e86a" :answer-field/choices ["q-107311/selection/7ed8ed7aff999b62180a1081cd49990f18ba6bec256be9e62a2b2d4b9385e86a" "q-107311/selection/79982823ad55938a9f7236929d695367b3b673bd6678f2f03e0ef53b14d590c3" "q-107311/selection/bdf02b857655926a707d4d030c1f998d371456a097454b3311c924b538df362f" "q-107311/selection/c2520e5f3e1d873a1f529de0067352587e320f2eb5c11f36a10589afa8fe72ac" "q-107311/selection/c5f992953d152b5e7cb0fdf7ddd228b1af5e5a8d4203d37d6fefbec1fad7aef9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107311" :question/id #uuid "8149d895-4d28-5f53-a40f-8127811ddb46" :question/math-academy-id "q-107311" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =29$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =29,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-29[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-18{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-107311/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107311"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80327/selection/3b50e10a129d48a005a40f7c9e3d5247bfee2a095c5a52be931fce99db9a2386" :answer/id #uuid "8f8218a1-f9f9-5aa9-90e2-7a94f2f12926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80327/selection/d896831607382a2d299fb205dca0c11c984b56e667e98df6d3b0d38b2b7a4a9a" :answer/id #uuid "40fb2811-0235-5d57-a96f-17c422b38b5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80327/selection/8057d85402e266b8bee87561709e1f61b04ba709dad036ca4a8f8b7e86d77da9" :answer/id #uuid "dbcfeb36-b8d0-5bd5-bc52-211e595d8ed7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80327/selection/fa8c2b1bc157effbadf768bee4520608730f656e923a7943f2032ed4ce7d301c" :answer/id #uuid "df42994d-e1d5-5673-8503-41b64e8df2c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80327/selection/4d07cc182de52d5460501a99da3ad3804bd14140e0d87a078c27adf4066e703f" :answer/id #uuid "ccc2ce48-a1fd-5c30-afcf-ba19d0e5c660" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80327/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb27570-187b-5e7b-a8df-b6c683603976" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80327/selection/3b50e10a129d48a005a40f7c9e3d5247bfee2a095c5a52be931fce99db9a2386" :answer-field/choices ["q-80327/selection/3b50e10a129d48a005a40f7c9e3d5247bfee2a095c5a52be931fce99db9a2386" "q-80327/selection/d896831607382a2d299fb205dca0c11c984b56e667e98df6d3b0d38b2b7a4a9a" "q-80327/selection/8057d85402e266b8bee87561709e1f61b04ba709dad036ca4a8f8b7e86d77da9" "q-80327/selection/fa8c2b1bc157effbadf768bee4520608730f656e923a7943f2032ed4ce7d301c" "q-80327/selection/4d07cc182de52d5460501a99da3ad3804bd14140e0d87a078c27adf4066e703f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80327" :question/id #uuid "0b431b2f-3f9d-53d8-a4fe-6fcd446daa8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80327" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80327/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80327"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5484"] :question/problem "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/c3f5f7169fd10d5710a95d0eab9c290faa04a544ac646a2ebcd24aa6b7e5f714.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/69c5e6ae96062d7c8e289e1f0070ab3874b15d0b42bb9300a50b9315723cfd8f.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=-5x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/f8c4aba8e8fb922f08741deb8df0fe4f47c64c29b647fec7da2b0ce1b4367225.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/5f7d229b6636bbfbe8eab4bdb7a6b8e72bb21c9cc9e66b1b4d607fd0e5e352e0.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5483"] :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a37420b7df40cfb806fadac008680a2e7346b1b2dea5d89a4baa912ab0bedb14.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/65b8ab3a3e0506afb1a0c3a7a928e21b98bcda313bafcbcfdb5b02771dd91693.png)\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a5098e5100bed7d15053e81b1127a774b828e30473718c6fc3fe73444eca3148.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5485"] :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/10510533350f1e77e4918cb4aff9edafe5ced005efc845becc8c1558bdd62811.png)\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/97e9cc73b2d39b924618c1372bf680ebf7d4374d1cf4fa9c3bc7e9afc05b06ea.png)\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a01037d3f9d9e090010a555fa0e14d6bce72da48c59bd74b0f7dad6afcee71ba.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/34313b24e58011d8be4790d57cd57d4800944903ff16dd20068b45bab2a7d261.png)\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/cb7300ef2064945937c27944e51032ade506ea16008e22f1a6ab54c5c647812c.png)\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/f6638f602a661865b1940f1409a20611c08f38821e2abcb204a4b38dd3a7adaa.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
